 const dec=[['Relaxation',h2-h1,0,h2-h1,dRl],['Shrinkage',h3-h2,f3-h1,(h3-h2)-(f3-h1),dSh],['Creep (incl. k9)',h4-h3,f4-f3,(h4-h3)-(f4-f3),dCr]];
 let html=`<h3>5 · Creep hog split – direct strain effect vs tendon-force change (comments 1 and 8), bare girder at ${lab}</h3><div class="w"><table><thead><tr><th class="l">Effect</th><th>Total Δ hog (mm)</th><th>Direct concrete strain effect, force held (mm)</th><th>Tendon-force change and bonded-steel restraint (mm)</th><th>Δ tendon force (kN)</th></tr></thead><tbody>${dec.map(a=>`<tr><td class="l">${a[0]}</td><td><b>${g(a[1])}</b></td><td>${g(a[2])}</td><td>${g(a[3])}</td><td>${gd(a[4])}</td></tr>`).join('')}<tr><td class="l"><b>Sum</b></td><td><b>${g(h4-h1)}</b></td><td>${g(dec[1][2]+dec[2][2])}</td><td>${g(dec[0][3]+dec[1][3]+dec[2][3])}</td><td>${gd(dRl+dSh+dCr)}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
 <p class="ref">“Force held”: the same time-step model with the strands acting as a constant external force (post-transfer value, no losses, no steel stiffness), so only concrete creep and shrinkage strain act. The difference to the full model is the effect of the force reduction and of the bonded steel restraining the concrete. Creep amplifies the sustained prestress and self-weight curvature (direct effect, upward) while the creep-induced force loss pulls it back (negative term) – the loss is a separate, opposing effect and is not added to the creep hog. Relaxation is a force loss by definition. The held-force run omits the steel area from the net section (about ${f(100*r.net.sA/p.A,1)} % of the gross area).</p>`;
